WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.195 --> 00:00:03.675
An MCP client and server talk in JSON-RPC

2
00:00:03.675 --> 00:00:04.695
messages.

3
00:00:04.695 --> 00:00:06.250
We'll follow three exchanges,

4
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:09.575
in order, until a tool actually runs.

5
00:00:09.995 --> 00:00:12.433
The client is the app hosting the model.

6
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:14.458
The server owns the tools.

7
00:00:14.458 --> 00:00:17.650
Messages travel as JSON-RPC over standard

8
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.400
input and output, or over HTTP.

9
00:00:21.545 --> 00:00:23.045
First comes the handshake,

10
00:00:23.045 --> 00:00:25.525
where both sides agree on a protocol version

11
00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:26.932
and what they support.

12
00:00:26.932 --> 00:00:29.645
Then the client asks what tools exist.

13
00:00:29.645 --> 00:00:32.000
Last, it calls one and gets a result

14
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.200
back.

15
00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:35.295
The client speaks first.

16
00:00:35.295 --> 00:00:38.012
Its request carries a protocol version and its

17
00:00:38.012 --> 00:00:39.900
own capabilities.

18
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.162
The server replies with the version it will

19
00:00:42.162 --> 00:00:44.120
use and what it offers.

20
00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.795
Here it says it has tools,

21
00:00:45.795 --> 00:00:47.225
and that it will tell the client when

22
00:00:47.225 --> 00:00:49.075
that list changes.

23
00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:51.370
Then the client sends a notification.

24
00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:52.450
It has no id,

25
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.320
so no reply is expected.

26
00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.700
It just means: ready.

27
00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.495
The list request has no parameters.

28
00:00:59.495 --> 00:01:00.970
Its id is two,

29
00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:02.520
and the reply will echo it,

30
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.775
so the client can match answers to questions.

31
00:01:05.895 --> 00:01:08.395
The reply describes each tool with a name,

32
00:01:08.395 --> 00:01:10.750
a description and a JSON Schema for its

33
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:11.632
input.

34
00:01:11.632 --> 00:01:14.032
The client passes these to the model.

35
00:01:14.032 --> 00:01:16.395
The model never sees the wire format,

36
00:01:16.395 --> 00:01:18.625
only these definitions.

37
00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:21.370
The model decides to use a tool.

38
00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:23.362
The client turns that into a call with

39
00:01:23.362 --> 00:01:26.975
the tool name and arguments matching the schema.

40
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.638
The server runs the code and returns content

41
00:01:29.638 --> 00:01:30.482
blocks.

42
00:01:30.482 --> 00:01:33.675
Text is common, but images and resources also

43
00:01:33.675 --> 00:01:34.825
work.

44
00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.670
A failing tool still gets a normal result,

45
00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:39.807
with the error flag set to true,

46
00:01:39.807 --> 00:01:41.600
so the model can read the failure and

47
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:42.607
react.

48
00:01:42.607 --> 00:01:45.883
Protocol-level problems, like an unknown tool name,

49
00:01:45.883 --> 00:01:49.575
come back as a JSON-RPC error instead.

50
00:01:50.045 --> 00:01:52.625
Four things carry the whole exchange.

51
00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.145
The handshake happens once per connection.

52
00:01:55.145 --> 00:01:57.807
Discovery tells the model what it can do.

53
00:01:57.807 --> 00:02:01.500
Execution is the only step with side effects.

54
00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.912
And the ids let many requests be in

55
00:02:03.912 --> 00:02:06.600
flight at once without confusion.
